### Description

#5762 made destruction allocation-free so that "destroying a value never allocates and never calls `std::terminate` because an allocation failed" (#5135). That holds for the default base class, but not for a `CustomBaseClass`: `json_value::destroy_container()` and `destroy_walk()` tear a container down through a stand-in declared as a full `basic_json` (`basic_json cur;`), and a default-constructed `basic_json` default-constructs the base class. So

1. every destroyed array or object default-constructs and destroys one base object that belongs to no value (four per level in `destroy_walk()`, i.e. for values deeper than the recursion limit), and
2. a base class whose default constructor allocates (a `std::string`, `std::vector` or `shared_ptr` member with a non-empty default) makes destruction allocate again inside the `noexcept` destructor; if that allocation fails, the process terminates, exactly what #5762 set out to prevent.

The documented requirement for `CustomBaseClass` is only "default-constructible, non-`final`" (`json_base_class_t.md`), so such a base is within contract. 3.12.0 terminated in the same situation for every base class (that was #5135); develop is better for non-allocating bases and unchanged for allocating ones.

### Reproduction steps

```sh
clang++ -std=c++17 -I include repro.cpp -o repro && ./repro     # or g++; same with -DJSON_DIAGNOSTICS=1
```

### Expected vs. actual results

Expected (and observed with `nlohmann::json`): `no throw at k=6: done`.

Actual with the base class below:

```
...
k=11 threw bad_alloc, fine
libc++abi: terminating due to uncaught exception of type std::bad_alloc: std::bad_alloc
```

(exit 134; gcc/libstdc++: `terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::bad_alloc'`). The copy succeeded; its destructor allocated the base class's string for the stand-in, and that allocation failed inside `data::~data() noexcept`.

A counting base class (every constructor increments a counter) shows the stand-ins directly. Destroying a parsed `{"a":[1,2,3],"b":{"c":true}}` (8 values):

| | default ctors | moves | dtors |
|---|---|---|---|
| develop ddc34f546a | 1 | 0 | 9 |
| 3.12.0 | 0 | 14 | 22 |

(3.12.0 moved every element into a temporary `std::vector`, which also allocated; #5762 replaced the moves with one default construction per container.)

### Minimal code example

```cpp
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <new>
#include <string>
#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
static long counter = 0, fail_at = 0;
void* operator new(std::size_t n)
{
    if (fail_at > 0 && ++counter == fail_at) { throw std::bad_alloc(); }
    void* p = std::malloc(n ? n : 1); if (!p) { throw std::bad_alloc(); } return p;
}
void operator delete(void* p) noexcept { std::free(p); }
void operator delete(void* p, std::size_t) noexcept { std::free(p); }
struct metadata { std::string note = "a note that is too long for the small string buffer of std::string"; };
using json = nlohmann::json::with_base_class_t<metadata>;
int main()
{
    const json src = {{"a", 1}, {"b", {1, 2, 3}}};
    for (long k = 1; k < 1000; ++k)
    {
        counter = 0; fail_at = k;
        try { { json copy = src; } fail_at = 0; std::printf("no throw at k=%ld: done\n", k); return 0; }
        catch (const std::bad_alloc&) { fail_at = 0; std::printf("k=%ld threw bad_alloc, fine\n", k); }
    }
}
```

### Error messages

`libc++abi: terminating due to uncaught exception of type std::bad_alloc` from `data::~data()` → `json_value::destroy_owned()` → `destroy_container()` → `basic_json::basic_json(std::nullptr_t)` → `metadata::metadata()`.

### Compiler and operating system

Apple clang 17 / libc++ (macOS 26) and gcc 16 / libstdc++ (Debian, Docker); plain and `JSON_DIAGNOSTICS=1`.

### Library version

develop at ddc34f546a (stand-in since #5762, 2026-10-06; the terminate itself also in 3.12.0 and earlier through the destructor's vector, #5135).

### Validation

- [x] The bug also occurs if the latest version from the [`develop`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/tree/develop) branch is used.
- [x] I can successfully [compile and run the unit tests](https://github.com/nlohmann/json#execute-unit-tests).

### Root cause

[`include/nlohmann/json.hpp:863-887`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a/include/nlohmann/json.hpp#L863-L887) `destroy_container()` (`basic_json cur;` at line 880) and [`destroy_walk()`](https://github.com/nlohmann/json/blob/ddc34f546a/include/nlohmann/json.hpp#L949-L1005) (`basic_json cur/prev/gp/tmp;` at lines 951, 954, 979, 1002), both `noexcept`, reached from `data::~data() noexcept` (line 6233). The stand-in only needs `m_type` and `m_value`, but it is a complete `basic_json`, whose constructor default-constructs `json_base_class_t`.

### Suggested fix

Use `data` (the struct holding `m_type` and `m_value`, no base class) as the stand-in: forward-declare `struct data;` before `union json_value`, change `take`, `has_no_children`, `all_children_leaves`, `walk_child`, `pop_walk_child`, `free_container`, `destroy_bounded` and `destroy_walk` to take `data&` (pass `child.m_data` / `child.second.m_data` for real children, return `array->back().m_data` from `walk_child`), and replace the `basic_json` locals with `data` locals (`data cur; cur.m_type = t; swap(cur.m_value, *this);`). Verified on a header copy: the reproducer above finishes (also with `-std=c++11 -DJSON_DIAGNOSTICS=1`), and a counting base class sees zero default constructions in every destroy case, with destructor counts equal to the number of values.

Tests: a counting `CustomBaseClass` asserting no constructions during destruction, and an allocating base class under the fail-point allocator from `unit-allocator.cpp`.

Related, not part of this issue: `basic_json(basic_json&&) noexcept` (line 2665) moves the base class under an unconditional `noexcept`, while copy assignment and `swap()` condition theirs on the base class; a base whose move constructor can throw terminates there too.

---
*Found during an allocation-failure bug hunt on develop; written by Claude Code on behalf of @nlohmann.*

